{-
   # extensions: avx2

   A lane above the low 128 bits, which is the one thing about the wide tier that no
   width-independent fixture can assert.

   **Separate from `VecWide.yana` because a lane index is checked where it is written.**
   `constantLane` in simd.cpp rejects `lane(v, 5)` on a four-lane vector during resolution, so the
   index cannot be guarded behind a run-time `if lanes > 4` - the guard is a branch and the check is
   not. A fixture naming lane 5 therefore has to be one that is *only* ever compiled at a width where
   lane 5 exists, which means no JavaScript half: `targetVectorBytes` answers 16 for that target
   whatever the directive says.

   What it exercises: every lane instruction AMD64 has names a lane inside one 128-bit register,
   whatever the prefix says the operation's width is. So a lane above the fourth is unreachable until
   the half holding it has been brought down with `vextracti128` - and a *write* to one has to put
   that half back with `vinserti128`, because `vpinsrd` is a VEX instruction and zeroes everything
   above the register it writes. Writing a lane of the low half in place would silently clear the
   upper half of the vector, which is why both halves go through the same three instructions.

   The reads are asserted against lanes the write did *not* touch as well as the one it did, since a
   sequence that lost the untouched half would still answer correctly about the lane it wrote. That
   is the shape the first version of this expansion got wrong: it took its scratch from the register
   the operand had been reloaded into, and answered a vector whose upper half was zero - which
   `horizontalMax` could not see and `horizontalMin` could.
-}

-- Mutable, so the folder cannot see through it and the lanes are built at run time.
let &seed = 3 :: Int

fn main() -> Int:
    let base = splat(seed) :: Vec(Int)

    -- A write into the upper half, read back beside the lanes around it.
    let high = withLane(base, 5, 40)
    if lane(high, 5) != 40 then return -1
    if lane(high, 4) != 3 then return -2
    if lane(high, 7) != 3 then return -3
    if lane(high, 0) != 3 then return -4

    -- And one into the lower half, which needs the same round trip for the opposite reason: the
    -- insert would zero the upper half if the half were not put back.
    let low = withLane(high, 1, 20)
    if lane(low, 1) != 20 then return -5
    if lane(low, 5) != 40 then return -6
    if lane(low, 6) != 3 then return -7

    -- The reductions over a vector whose lanes are genuinely different, which is what makes the
    -- butterfly's cross-half level answer rather than merely run: 40 is in the upper half and 20 in
    -- the lower, so a tree that never crossed would answer one of them.
    if horizontalMax(low) != 40 then return -8
    if horizontalMin(low) != 3 then return -9

    -- 3*6 + 20 + 40, which reads every lane exactly once.
    if horizontalSum(low) != 78 then return -10

    return lane(low, 5) * 10 + lane(low, 1)
